WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.760
When building a React Native app, it is easy to view your sandboxed job-escript environment

2
00:00:05.760 --> 00:00:10.240
as entirely decoupled from the host device's rigid security model.

3
00:00:10.240 --> 00:00:16.160
In practice, Android's native permission system maps directly on to React Native's architecture.

4
00:00:16.160 --> 00:00:21.440
Every underlying mechanic, from the runtime prompts and dangerous category classifications,

5
00:00:21.440 --> 00:00:27.200
to the rationale dialogues shown to the user, executes exactly as it does in Native Kotlin.

6
00:00:27.360 --> 00:00:32.480
Developer friction typically originates at the fragmented API surface of the JavaScript bridge

7
00:00:32.480 --> 00:00:37.200
that interacts with these native rules. Depending on your stack, you might interface with this

8
00:00:37.200 --> 00:00:42.640
bridge through the bare React Native permissions Android module, unified Expo feature modules,

9
00:00:42.640 --> 00:00:47.760
or third-party packages. Regardless of the path you choose, the result is the same.

10
00:00:47.760 --> 00:00:53.040
Developers trigger silent application failures by ignoring the edge cases of the Android OS state

11
00:00:53.040 --> 00:00:57.680
machine. These failures usually cluster around two specific events,

12
00:00:57.680 --> 00:01:03.680
the mishandling of permanent user denials, and running a foul of modern granular media constraints

13
00:01:03.680 --> 00:01:09.120
on newer Android versions. This flow chart represents the complete patterned state machine.

14
00:01:09.120 --> 00:01:13.920
It maps out the exact architectural solution to handle these exact edge cases,

15
00:01:13.920 --> 00:01:18.640
routing initial requests to either granted, denied, or permanently denied states.

16
00:01:18.960 --> 00:01:23.760
Reliable implementation relies on mapping these specific Android state transitions

17
00:01:23.760 --> 00:01:28.640
directly into the application architecture. Before we can trigger any runtime code,

18
00:01:28.640 --> 00:01:33.760
we have to start at the foundational layer. Imperative JavaScript code is entirely powerless

19
00:01:33.760 --> 00:01:39.520
without declarative OS permissions to find first. This side-by-side code layout shows how we establish

20
00:01:39.520 --> 00:01:45.920
those rules. On the left, in bare React Native projects, the Android Manifest.xml file acts as the

21
00:01:45.920 --> 00:01:51.040
absolute source of truth for the operating system. In the Expo-managed workflow shown on the

22
00:01:51.040 --> 00:01:57.680
right, you define these same requirements in your app.json file. Expo then dynamically generates

23
00:01:57.680 --> 00:02:04.080
the underlying manifest at build time. The OS groups these requests into specific categories.

24
00:02:04.080 --> 00:02:09.280
Normal permissions, like internet access, are granted automatically at install time and require

25
00:02:09.280 --> 00:02:14.560
no further action. The category that triggers our runtime state machine is labeled protection

26
00:02:14.640 --> 00:02:20.320
dangerous. This classification covers access to critical user data and hardware vectors,

27
00:02:20.320 --> 00:02:26.800
specifically the camera, location, storage, and contacts. Android prevents any runtime state change

28
00:02:26.800 --> 00:02:32.000
in JavaScript for a permission that was not declared in the manifest at install time. Once the

29
00:02:32.000 --> 00:02:37.760
manifest is set, we return to the runtime state machine. This translation matrix maps native

30
00:02:37.760 --> 00:02:43.440
Kotlin package manager results directly to their JavaScript equivalents. First, the bridge maps the

31
00:02:43.440 --> 00:02:49.760
native permission granted success state directly to the JavaScript granted constant. Next, the native

32
00:02:49.760 --> 00:02:56.320
permission denied state maps to the JavaScript denied constant. This represents a temporary block

33
00:02:56.320 --> 00:03:02.240
where the user said no, but the app is still allowed to ask again. The third state is where most

34
00:03:02.240 --> 00:03:08.560
architectural failures occur, permanent denial. Look at the complex native property on the left.

35
00:03:08.560 --> 00:03:14.720
Native Android determines a permanent denial using a negative Boolean check. If the OS decides it

36
00:03:14.720 --> 00:03:19.440
should not show a request rationale dialogue, it means the user permanently blocked the app.

37
00:03:20.080 --> 00:03:25.440
React native abstracts this clunky Boolean logic into a single never-ask again constant on the

38
00:03:25.440 --> 00:03:31.280
JavaScript side. If your app enters this state, you are behaviorally locked out. Any subsequent

39
00:03:31.280 --> 00:03:36.160
permission requests you execute in JavaScript will automatically fail without ever showing a dialogue

40
00:03:36.160 --> 00:03:41.360
to the user. Your application architecture must actively listen for the specific constant.

41
00:03:41.360 --> 00:03:47.360
When detected, the only valid response is to display custom UI, redirecting the user out of your

42
00:03:47.360 --> 00:03:54.320
app and into the Android OS settings menu. Explicitly handling this never-ask again node is the only

43
00:03:54.320 --> 00:04:00.240
way to prevent users from falling into a silent, inescapable denial loop. Even with the result nodes

44
00:04:00.240 --> 00:04:06.320
perfectly mapped, Android 13, API level 33, introduced a disruption that breaks the standard

45
00:04:06.320 --> 00:04:12.160
flow. This diagram illustrates how Android 13 deprecated the monolithic read external storage

46
00:04:12.160 --> 00:04:18.240
permission, splitting it into specific asset categories. These are scoped media permissions. Instead of

47
00:04:18.240 --> 00:04:23.760
asking for global access to the entire file system, apps must now make granular requests for images,

48
00:04:23.760 --> 00:04:29.040
video, or audio. If you ignore this architectural shift and request the old global storage permission

49
00:04:29.040 --> 00:04:34.560
on a device running API 33 or higher, the OS will automatically and silently deny the request.

50
00:04:34.560 --> 00:04:40.880
To fix this, you have to implement conditional logic. By checking platform.version in React native,

51
00:04:40.880 --> 00:04:46.400
you can branch your request type based on the exact OS the user is running. A robust state

52
00:04:46.400 --> 00:04:52.000
machine must query the host environment's version to ensure legacy permissions aren't sent to modern

53
00:04:52.000 --> 00:04:57.840
systems. With the OS theory mapped and the edge cases accounted for, we can physically trigger this

54
00:04:57.840 --> 00:05:03.760
state machine in a code base. You have a few raw tools available. Bear workflows use permissions

55
00:05:03.760 --> 00:05:10.400
Android, while specific Expo modules rely on request permissions async. But the industry standard

56
00:05:10.400 --> 00:05:16.480
for bridging these distinct API surfaces is the React native permissions package. The primary

57
00:05:16.480 --> 00:05:22.000
advantage of this library is unification. It allows you to use the exact Android state machine we've

58
00:05:22.000 --> 00:05:27.360
just built, while standardizing the API to work with iOS's inherently different life cycle.

59
00:05:28.000 --> 00:05:33.120
The most effective implementation strategy is constructing a custom React hook designed

60
00:05:33.120 --> 00:05:39.280
strictly for permission management. Looking back at our state machine flum chart, the hook manages

61
00:05:39.280 --> 00:05:45.760
the entire life cycle. It initializes the status check, fires the request if undetermined, supplies

62
00:05:45.760 --> 00:05:50.400
a rationale on the first denial, and prompts the settings redirect on permanent denial.

63
00:05:51.040 --> 00:05:56.960
Isolating this logic inside a custom hook keeps your visual UI components completely clean of

64
00:05:56.960 --> 00:06:03.200
OS level concerns and prevents race conditions on the native bridge. When that hook finally resolves

65
00:06:03.200 --> 00:06:10.400
to the granted state, it signifies full authorization. Your app transforms from a sandboxed UI into a

66
00:06:10.400 --> 00:06:16.640
privileged client, fully capable of interacting with the device hardware. With those privileges secured,

67
00:06:16.640 --> 00:06:22.080
the immediate next step in the development life cycle is to bind our application directly to the

68
00:06:22.080 --> 00:06:28.480
device's camera hardware. Next, we'll break down the native Android camera X concepts and map exactly

69
00:06:28.480 --> 00:06:36.960
how they translate to react native vision systems.

